Build: -O3 -march=native SUCCEEDED.  /tmp/crypto-build.log was created empty
(script redirects compiler stderr there); no "native build failed, falling back
to -O3" message appeared and no fallback binary was built.

Note on the cpu_flags line: crypto.c prints only a whitelist of SIMD-relevant
flags {sse2 ssse3 avx avx2 avx512f asimd neon sve sve2 aes pmull}.  The printed
"sse2 ssse3 aes avx" therefore means "none of avx2/avx512f/ARM names present",
not that SSE4.2/FMA/BMI1 are missing -- see the full /proc/cpuinfo line above.

Note on x4 vs scalar: on this host the 4-lane vector path is SLOWER than scalar
at every round count (ChaCha20 618 vs 697 MB/s, ChaCha12 935-944 vs 1038,
ChaCha8 1256-1277 vs 1373; about -10% to -11%).  The v4u type is 16 bytes
(128 bit, the widest this core has) and vrotl is shift+or rather than a vector
rotate instruction, so the vector path pays the same ALU work per byte as four
independent scalar blocks while adding shuffles.  This is the opposite direction
from HND0 (QEMU, sse2-only), where x4 was ~2.3x faster than scalar.

